---
import ArticleLayout from "@layouts/ArticleLayout.astro";
---

<ArticleLayout
  title="Solar State of Charge"
  description="An n8n automation that reads a Victron installation every hour and sends the battery state of charge to Telegram."
  subtitle="One number, every hour, wherever I am."
  eyebrow="2026 · Automation / n8n"
  tags="Automation · n8n · Victron VRM · Telegram"
>
  <p class="lead">
    A small scheduled workflow. It asks the Victron VRM API for the current
    battery state of charge, then sends it to a Telegram chat. Four nodes, no
    server to keep running.
  </p>

  <figure class="workflow-shot">
    <a href="/assets/solar-state-of-charge/workflow.png">
      <img
        src="/assets/solar-state-of-charge/workflow.png"
        alt="The n8n canvas showing four connected nodes: Schedule Trigger, HTTP Request, Edit Fields and Send a text message"
        width="1173"
        height="545"
        loading="lazy"
      />
    </a>
    <figcaption>
      The workflow: hourly trigger, one API call, one field, one message. Open
      the full screenshot.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <section>
    <h2>Why bother</h2>
    <p>
      The solar installation reports everything, but opening a monitoring
      dashboard for one number is more effort than it is worth. A message in a
      chat I already read answers the question without a new app or a login.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>The four steps</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <strong>Schedule Trigger</strong> — runs the workflow every hour.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>HTTP Request</strong> — calls
        <code>
          vrmapi.victronenergy.com/v2/installations/&lt;id&gt;/diagnostics
        </code>
        with an <code>X-Authorization: Token …</code> header.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Edit Fields</strong> — picks the state of charge out of the
        response. VRM returns a long <code>records</code> array, so the field is
        read by index and renamed to <code>Charge</code>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Telegram</strong> — sends <code>{"State of Charge: "}…</code>
        with the charge value to a chat ID.
      </li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>The only awkward part</h2>
    <p>
      The diagnostics endpoint returns every record for the installation, not a
      named object. There is no key to look up, so the node reads
      <code>records[175]</code> and takes its <code>formattedValue</code>. It
      works, but it is brittle: if Victron adds or reorders a record, the index
      moves and the message reports the wrong number.
    </p>
    <p>
      The next version should find the record by its code or description instead
      of its position. The trigger also carries{" "}
      <em>continue on regular output</em>, so a failed API call does not stop
      the workflow.
    </p>
  </section>

  <aside class="project-status">
    Working automation · The API token and chat ID are configured in n8n
    credentials and are not published here.
  </aside>
  <a class="text-link" href="/journal/bus-351/">
    Next automation: Bus 351 ↗
  </a>
</ArticleLayout>

